# Clean Code — Documentation and Formatting

## Formatting and structure

- Optimize for scanning and top-down reading.
- Group related statements together and separate unrelated concepts with whitespace.
- Keep methods and expressions readable without excessive horizontal complexity.
- Follow the repository's `.editorconfig`, analyzer configuration, formatter, and established member ordering before inventing new style rules.
- Do not reformat unrelated code during a functional change.
- Keep high-level methods easy to find before low-level implementation details when that matches the project's style.

## Comments

- Prefer expressive code over comments.
- Comment the **why**, not the obvious **what**.
- Keep comments short, precise, and synchronized with the implementation.
- Remove stale or misleading comments when touching the affected code.
- Do not use comments to justify avoidable complexity.

## Public APIs

- Document public APIs when the project or analyzer requires XML documentation.
- Describe contract, important constraints, side effects, exceptions, and non-obvious behavior rather than restating parameter names.
- Do not add documentation to every private method unless it explains a non-obvious invariant.

## Quality gates

- Before finishing, use the repository's existing formatter/analyzers when available.
- Do not suppress compiler or analyzer warnings merely to make a change pass. Prefer fixing the underlying issue; if suppression is genuinely required, keep it narrow and documented.
